Let the machine's frames be read on a boundary they are actually on

The backtracking stack puts a frame wherever the machine's own frame sizes
land it, and those sizes come from the language data: ca_size, size_b0, and in
vback's case two a size worked out to be explicitly odd. So a delta_frame is
routinely at a two-byte boundary, while the struct declared over it says its
int32_t fields are word aligned.

On x86 and on ARM64 the difference never shows, because an unaligned load or
store is simply done. On ARM32 it is not. A compiler that believes the declared
alignment is entitled to move two adjacent fields with one multi-word
instruction, and LDM and STM fault on anything but a word boundary whatever
SCTLR says. Both happen here: bspush_ca_scan's eight-byte copy became STMIB and
vback's read of slot->value beside slot->length became LDMIB, and each faulted
with SIGBUS on the first rule the engine ran.

Two things, and neither changes a layout. delta_frame is marked packed, which
says what is already true -- every offset in it is written out, so there is no
padding for packing to take away -- and takes away only the compiler's right to
assume an alignment the frame has not got. A static assertion beside it holds
the size and both offsets to what they were, so a layout that did move would
stop the build rather than the engine. And the nine eight-byte copies of the
scan position and the loop tags go through a type of alignment one, since those
move between structures rather than inside one and the same reasoning applies
at both ends.

Verified. test/matrix.sh check enus, against the baselines recorded on x86, so
both hashes are compared -- the samples and the answers the probe reported:

    x86, mingw, RULES=bytecode          98 cases, none moved
    aarch64, Pixel 9a                   98 cases, none moved
    armv7a, Pixel Watch 4               98 cases, none moved

The armv7a column is the new one. Before this it did not reach the end of the
first utterance: SIGBUS, BUS_ADRALN, in bspush_ca_scan. The other two are what
says this costs the platforms that already worked nothing.

The aarch64 and armv7a runs are cli/probe.c built with the NDK and driven on
the device over adb, with a shim standing in for the probe so test/matrix.sh
runs unchanged. Both were made with the delta_low change from the patch before
this one also applied. That one touches different files and the two do not
depend on each other to build, but it is exercised on 32-bit as well as on
64-bit -- delta_syms_bind runs either way and EVV_AT and EVV_REF are identity
casts rather than nothing where a pointer already fits a value -- so it is not
true to say armv7a does not need it, and the numbers above were not measured
without it.

